#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128846 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129269 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e290e8b2-7766-55ff-95da-99a3430b18e3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Jacobian of an Inverse Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "b22d04e0-8c6a-507a-9d76-ab05adb32b10" :question/math-academy-id "e-11517" :question/problem "Consider the transformation $\\mathbf{T}:(u,v,w) \\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)).$ The inverse transformation $\\mathbf T^{-1}(x,y,z)\\to(u(x,y,z), v(x,y,z), w(x,y,z))$ is given by\n![](images/f8/f82cf760dee34626c290e29420fbaddb4ef254180087d05bb6fb883f2dc32e75.svg)\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T.$\n\n*Hint: You can assume that $x>0$, $y \\ne 0.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n![](images/1c/1c2567cd1c2cf28ed17b6dc445025cb60061dc7d7d6c78c960be77f8866ef4a7.svg)\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\dfrac{\\partial (u, v, w)}{\\partial (x, y, z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v, w)}{\\partial (x, y, z)} \\right)^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nu(x,y,z) = x^2, \\qquad \nv(x,y,z) = xy, \\qquad\nw(x,y,z) = yz.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =2x & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =y & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =x & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =z & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2x & 0 & 0 \\\\ y & x & 0 \\\\ 0 & z & y\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2x|\\begin{aligned}x & 0 \\\\ z & y\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2x(xy-0) \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting down $\\dfrac{\\partial (u, v, w)}{\\partial (x, y, z)}$ in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}y & =2\\cdot x\\cdot (xy) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\sqrt{u}\\cdot v \\\\  & =2v\\sqrt{u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2v\\sqrt{u}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128847 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129270 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b4a31c6-01be-5b15-88f5-9150f350e944" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Critical Points of a Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "b16845a2-5f66-524a-90ea-37f8a8186f8f" :question/math-academy-id "e-11518" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ where $\\mathbf T$ is defined as\n\n![](images/22/22690753019e406703952ae0c2a79450764b5ff42c758a03b2875a9d428d9e78.svg)" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n![](images/be/be4c52a967cf6430f1d365ebb9b3a3a20bc852ac6422ed51e01dc5b39055c816.svg)\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w) =w-u^3, \\qquad \ny(u,v,w) = 2uv, \\qquad\nz(u,v,w) = u^2+v.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =-3{u}^{2} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =2v & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}-3{u}^{2} & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2v & 2u & 0 \\\\ 2u & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-3{u}^{2}|\\begin{aligned}2u & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|+1|\\begin{aligned}2v & 2u \\\\ 2u & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-3{u}^{2}(0-0)+1(2v-4{u}^{2}) \\\\  & =2(v-2{u}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} =0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ 2(v-2{u}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ v-2{u}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $v-2u^2 = 0$ defines the parabolic cylinder $v=2u^2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128848 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129271 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d433063f-f145-5bf4-8bd4-8d5e5b36b3d8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Jacobian of a Three-Dimensional Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "66d757d2-f2ad-5f0d-b4f1-8d426f4c8351" :question/math-academy-id "e-11579" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ that maps the Cartesian $uvw$-space onto the Cartesian $xyz$-space by means of the equations\n\n$$\nx = 2u-v,\\qquad\ny = u+3v+w,\\qquad\nz = u+2v-w.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n![](images/6e/6e94e780387ccbba3d8b6a6aaf5f00fbf362cd8163837a8ea78ad31c18f3b1b3.svg)\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} ,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =3 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =2 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}|-(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(-3-2)+(-1-1) \\\\  & =-10-2 \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406771 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129270 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b4a31c6-01be-5b15-88f5-9150f350e944" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Critical Points of a Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06cfa4e4-4978-5d07-b498-b67163d323ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $uw-$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406760} {:answer/id #uuid "47f0e73b-3fcc-5922-ac4c-5cbbb15dc078" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406762} {:answer/id #uuid "4cd27081-6df8-5fcc-87e8-0b763e12dd24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $vw-$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406763} {:answer/id #uuid "bdd4d236-b060-5654-b754-6a3374d3448b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $v=1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406766} {:answer/id #uuid "ddaabaf5-f0e8-54a9-9f5a-d6c4751feaf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $v=u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406768}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06cfa4e4-4978-5d07-b498-b67163d323ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $uw-$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406760} :answer-field/id #uuid "06a97003-66fd-5b44-944b-7b4ac3d8364b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406769}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "84951e54-c705-5b01-85eb-275b777e397c" :question/math-academy-id "q-161810" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}u+vw \\\\ {v}^{2} \\\\ w+v\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=u+vw,\\,y(u,v,w)={v}^{2},\\,z(u,v,w)=w+v.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =w & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =2v & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & w & v \\\\ 0 & 2v & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}2v & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(2v-0) \\\\  & =2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ 2v & =0 \\\\ v & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $v=0$ defines the plane $v=0,$ i.e., $uw$-plane."}]}
